{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '261,600p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"task-board m 'unlink(TASK-260218-def456, blocked_by=TASK-260218-abc123)'                    # remove dependency\ntask-board m 'check_item(TASK-260218-abc123, item=1)'                                       # check checklist item\ntask-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Write tests\")'                   # add checklist item\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Progress update\")'                        # append notes\ntask-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260218-abc123, text=\"Full replacement\", set=true)'             # replace notes\ntask-board m 'set_parent(TASK-260218-abc123, parent=STORY-260218-def456)'                   # move to new parent\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'  # create resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"revised\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design v2\")'  # replace resource\ntask-board m --dry-run 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # preview destructive op\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete(TASK-260218-abc123)'                                         # execute destructive op\n\n# Batching (semicolons)\ntask-board m 'assign(TASK-260218-abc123, agent=claude-opus); set_status(TASK-260218-abc123, status=development)'\n\n# Spawn tracked background runs (--role, --background, and --model are REQUIRED; Codex/Qwen also require --reasoning-effort)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction impl-guide.md\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8 --budget 5.0\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent qwen --model qwen3.7-plus --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-luna --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc123 --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark --reasoning-effort xhigh\ntask-board spawn status RUN-260320-abc123                                            # inspect tracked run state\ntask-board spawn wait RUN-260320-abc123                                              # block on tracked run state after RUN exists\ntask-board spawn events RUN-260320-abc123                                            # replay durable run events (supports --cursor/--follow)\ntask-board spawn watch RUN-260320-abc123                                             # follow durable run-event notifications\n\n# Auth (always local, never sent to server)\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_4lc6...                                        # store token in Keychain and select active user outside repo config\ntask-board login admin tb_UJTW... --remote https://board.example.com          # explicit remote bootstrap\ntask-board logout                                                              # remove selected user's token\ntask-board whoami                                                              # show remote URL + active user + token status\ntask-board switch-user admin                                                   # switch active user without touching versioned config\ntask-board q 'whoami()'                                                        # whoami via query DSL\ntask-board q 'project_config()'                                                # mode, board source, and effective spawn policy\ntask-board m 'login(user=sampleuser, token=tb_xxx)'                           # login via mutation DSL\ntask-board m 'switch_user(user=admin)'                                         # switch via mutation DSL\n\n# CLI utilities\ntask-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"                            # full-text search across board files (local only)\ntask-board validate                                        # check board structure (local only)\ntask-board repair-links                                    # list unsupported stored container links (local, read-only)\ntask-board repair-links STORY-260218-abc123 --confirm      # remove selected suspicious links\ntask-board tui                                             # interactive terminal dashboard\n```\n\n---\n\n## Task Board Structure\n\n### Hierarchy\n\n```\nEpic → Story → Task/Bug\n```\n\n- **Epic** = large feature / module\n- **Story** = specific functionality within epic\n- **Task** = atomic unit of work\n- **Bug** = defect tracked within a story\n\n### Naming Convention\n\n**Distributed IDs** — each element gets a unique ID based on date + random hash.\n\n```\nEPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n  STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n    TASK-260203-g7h8i9_audiorecorder-interface/\n    TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n    BUG-260203-m3n4o5_some-bug/\n```\n\nFormat: `{TYPE}-{YYMMDD}-{hash}_{kebab-case-name}`\n- `EPIC-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `STORY-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `TASK-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n- `BUG-YYMMDD-xxxxxx_name`\n\nWhere:\n- `YYMMDD` — creation date (year, month, day)\n- `xxxxxx` — 6-character base36 hash (collision-resistant)\n\nIDs are globally unique and work in parallel environments (no counter conflicts).\n\n### Directory Layout\n\n```\n.task-board/\n├── .resources/                   # Flat resource storage (managed by CLI)\n│   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9/       # Resources keyed by element ID\n│   │   ├── design.md             # Precondition: input for the task\n│   │   └── results.md            # Outcome: work product\n│   └── STORY-260203-d4e5f6/\n│       └── research.md\n├── EPIC-260203-a1b2c3_recording/\n│   ├── README.md                 # Epic description, scope, AC\n│   ├── progress.md               # Status, blockedBy, checklist, notes, resource refs\n│   ├── STORY-260203-d4e5f6_audio-capture/\n│   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   ├── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-g7h8i9_interface/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   ├── TASK-260203-j0k1l2_implementation/\n│   │   │   ├── README.md\n│   │   │   └── progress.md\n│   │   └── BUG-260203-m3n4o5_crash-on-start/\n│   │       ├── README.md\n│   │       └── progress.md\n│   └── STORY-260203-p6q7r8_amplitude/\n│       └── ...\n└── EPIC-260203-s9t0u1_storage/\n    └── ...\n```\n\nNo `system.md` needed — IDs are self-contained and don't require global counters.\n\n---\n\n## Statuses\n\n9 statuses: `backlog` → `analysis` → `to-dev` → `development` → `to-review` → `reviewing` → `done`. Plus `closed` and `blocked`.\n\nBefore a newly created Task/Bug enters `development`, set its estimate with\n`set_estimate(ID, estimate=estimated, scale=fibonacci, number=N)`. The start\ntransition returns a typed estimate-required error while the explicit value is\n`notEstimated`. Legacy Task/Bug files that have no Estimate section are\ngrandfathered and remain startable.\n\n`done` is accepted leaf work or computed container completion; `closed` is an\nexplicit administrative disposition. A story or epic may remain `closed` only\nwhile every direct and transitive descendant is `done` or `closed`.\n\nFor full status flow, auto-promotion/reopen, blocked vs is_blocked — see [`references/statuses.md`](references/statuses.md).\n\n---\n\n## File Formats\n\nEach element directory contains two files:\n- **README.md** — static description (what the element is, scope, acceptance criteria). Does not change during execution.\n- **progress.md** — dynamic lifecycle journal (status, Task/Bug estimate, assignment, timestamps, dependencies, checklist, notes). Updated throughout execution.\n\nBoth files use source-aware, injection-safe Markdown persistence. Unchanged\nbytes and custom sections are retained during rewrites; free-text payload fields\nround-trip byte-exact. Mutation boundaries normalize identifier-like fields\n(`AssignedTo`, `BlockedBy`, `Blocks`) by trimming boundary whitespace and reject\nempty or reserved `(none)` values with a typed error. Checklist text is rejected\nonly when empty-after-trim and is otherwise persisted verbatim.\n\nTask/bug dependencies are bidirectional ground truth. Story/epic ordering is derived from the live task DAG at read time; task links never materialize container side-effect links. Explicit stored container links remain manual state, while projections expose task-derived blockers separately.\n\nFor detailed format specifications, field descriptions, and examples, see [`references/file-formats.md`](references/file-formats.md).\n\n---\n\n## Resources & Bugs\n\nResources are linked to board elements via DSL mutations. Two types:\n- **Precondition** — inputs for the task (instructions, specs, design docs). Used by `task-board spawn` to compose agent prompts.\n- **Outcome** — work products (research results, implementation notes). Persisted for other agents/tasks to consume.\n\n```bash\n# Create resource\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Architecture design\")'\ntask-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"...\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings\")'\n\n# Replace existing resource\ntask-board m 'update_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_results.md, content=\"revised findings\", type=outcome, description=\"Research findings v2\")'\n\n# Delete resource\ntask-board m --confirm 'delete_resource(TASK-260218-abc123, name=TASK-260218-abc123_design.md)'\n```\n\nResources are stored flat in `.task-board/.resources/<elementID>/` and referenced from `progress.md`. Bugs live inside stories alongside tasks.\n\nFor resource workflow and bug tracking — see [`references/resources-and-bugs.md`](references/resources-and-bugs.md).\n\n---\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Full Development Cycle (MANDATORY)\n\nWhen the user asks to build/implement something, the agent MUST follow this end-to-end flow:\n\n#### Spec\n- Specs live in the `.spec/` directory (one or more `.md` files), not in a single `SPEC.md`\n- Create `.spec/` if it doesn't exist; add focused spec files per topic/feature (e.g. `.spec/auth.md`, `.spec/api.md`)\n- Clarify requirements with the user if anything is ambiguous\n- `.spec/` is the source of truth — all work traces back to it\n\n#### Plan on the Board\n- Create epics and stories: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n- Refine each element: query with `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`, clarify gaps, update via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\")'`\n- Perform spec-to-board decomposition inline as the Orchestrator; do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` merely to lay out a settled spec\n- No element should have placeholder text — description and AC must be complete and unambiguous\n\n#### Story Detailing, Phase Planning, Execution (MANDATORY)\n\nAfter the Orchestrator completes story detailing inline, use [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md) for phase planning, user approval, pre-launch checks, specialist execution, supervision, and visualization. Its legacy decomposition-spawn examples do not override this inline mandate and must not be used for settled-spec board layout.\n\nKey points:\n- **Story detailing before planning** — the Orchestrator creates and refines the required stories and tasks inline before phase planning\n- **No meta-delegated board layout** — do not create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely to translate a settled spec into board elements; reserve that role for genuine architecture decisions such as system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts\n- **Use `task-board q 'plan(...)'` as the only planning source** — default JSON returns nested phases and `criticalPath`; compact output returns flat `waves` and a single-line `criticalPath`. Unresolvable dependencies fail with a typed validation error instead of producing misleading phases. Agents never need a rendered graph to plan. Use `task-board plan --save` only to persist a human-readable snapshot; never build phases manually or write plan files by hand\n- **Dump plan to `.planning/`** — after planning, save to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. Use `task-board plan --save`\n- **User approval MANDATORY** — never start execution without user confirmation\n- **Pre-launch checklist** — model, status, assignment, concrete IDs, first command\n- **Coordinator is SUPERVISOR** — never writes code, only delegates to sub-agents\n\n#### Research / Analysis Tasks\n\nResearch workflow, resource linking, document structure, status flow — see [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md).\n\nKey points:\n- Research results are linked to board elements via `task-board m 'add_resource(..., type=outcome)'` on first write and `update_resource(...)` on revisions\n- Broadly useful research also persists in **`.research/`** directory (project-level)\n- Status flow: `backlog → analysis → to-review → reviewing → done` (skip `development`; after `to-review`, spawn an explicit reviewer such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`)\n- Always link research output as an outcome resource on the task, and include the task ID in the resource name\n\n### Iterative Refinement (for individual elements)\n\nWhen creating ANY element (epic/story/task/bug), the agent MUST:\n\n1. **Create** element via `task-board m 'create(type=..., name=..., description=\"...\")'`\n2. **Read** the element: `task-board q 'get(ID) { full }'`\n3. **Clarify** — if description is vague or has gaps, ask the user\n4. **Update** via `task-board m 'set_details(ID, description=\"...\", ac=\"...\")'`\n5. **Repeat** steps 2-4 until description is exhaustive\n6. Element is **ready** only when description and AC are complete and unambiguous\n\n### Project Start\n\n- Create elements: `task-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system, description=\"...\")'`\n- Create stories: `task-board m 'create(type=story, parent=EPIC-260218-abc, name=login)'`\n- Detail tasks: `task-board m 'create(type=task, parent=STORY-260218-abc, name=impl)'`\n- Set dependencies: `task-board m 'link(STORY-260218-def, blocked_by=STORY-260218-abc)'`\n- **Generate phases**: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-260218-abc, mode=children)'` — only after all stories have tasks and dependencies are set\n\n### Spawning Agents for Tasks\n\nThe **coordinator never writes code** — it delegates through tracked spawn commands with explicit role/model selection and runtime-affine agent selection.\n\nTreat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing spawn contract. The guidance below is the workflow summary agents should follow.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED.** Codex and Qwen spawns also require `--reasoning-effort`; Claude spawns do not accept it. Choose the role based on the task:\n\n`--agent` is optional by design. Canonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` is an enforced tagged union: `exclusive` pins one provider; `mixed` permits a normalized provider set and retains runtime affinity when it belongs to that set. An explicit `--agent` wins only within the configured set; an out-of-set explicit provider or affinity is refused before task lookup or spawn side effects and names the allowed providers. When the canonical system is absent, legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` retains its ceiling/executable-gated preference and affinity fallback; when both are absent, a Codex orchestrator session spawns a `codex` child and a Claude orchestrator session spawns a `claude` child. The implementation detects Codex from `CODEX_SESSION`, `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, or `CODEX_CI` and otherwise uses Claude.\n\nOptional `spawn.schedule.windows[]` overlays that base provider system during disjoint UTC windows. Each window is start-inclusive/end-exclusive, uses `HH:MM`, may cross midnight, and carries its own `preferred_agentic_system`; equal or overlapping bounds are invalid. The active window replaces the base system before provider and registered-model validation, while gaps use the base system or unrestricted runtime affinity when no base exists. Inspect `project_config().spawn.schedule` for the configured windows, `active_window`, and `base_preferred_agentic_system` before selecting a provider.\n\n### Goal-Capable Primary Sessions\n\nThe fixed primary-session UX is `agents-infra codex` for Codex and\n`agents-infra claude` for Claude. Both are fully capable of starting\ngoal-bound tracked owners through `task-board spawn --goal-scope`.\n\n- `agents-infra` remains board-agnostic: it has no `task-board` dependency and\n  does not create, discover, or preserve a caller goal plane.\n- `task-board codex` is an optional enhanced Codex primary session. It is never\n  required to own a goal-bound tracked run.\n- `CODEX_THREAD_ID`, `CODEX_SESSION`, and `CODEX_CI` select Codex affinity only;\n  they do not identify an endpoint or form an addressable caller goal plane.\n\n| Capability | `agents-infra codex` primary | `task-board codex` optional primary | `agents-infra claude` primary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Launch the initial orchestrator | Required Codex UX | Optional, not required UX | Required Claude UX |\n| Start a goal-bound tracked owner | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Bind the native goal before owner work | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Dedicated Codex app-server/thread | Fresh Claude process with native `/goal` |\n| Preserve an existing caller goal | Zero caller RPCs when plane is absent | Exact snapshot/restore when plane is reachable | Not applicable; no caller-session rewrite |\n| Query/upsert active goal, directives, acceptance, and recovery | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Durable `events`, `watch`, and `wait` observation | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Push terminal state into an active Codex thread | No | Yes | No Codex push; Claude harness background wait wakes the session |\n| Codex auto-continue nudges | No | Yes | Not a Codex capability |\n| Caller-session app-server RPC access | No supported endpoint | Yes | Not applicable |\n\nParity is behavioral, not mechanical: both providers create a fresh tracked\nowner, bind a native continuation condition before delivery work, and share the\nsame immutable revision, scope, directive, recovery, and acceptance contract.\nCodex uses a dedicated app-server thread; Claude uses a fresh `/goal` process.\nSee [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md)\nfor the operator contract.\n\n| Task type | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| Research, analysis, investigation | `--role researcher` |\n| Code implementation, bugfixes | `--role developer` |\n| Writing tests | `--role tester` |\n| Genuine architecture decisions: system-design tradeoffs or unresolved cross-module contracts | `--role solution-architect` |\n| Documentation | `--role doc-writer` |\n| Code review, quality check | `--role reviewer` |\n\n**Workflow:**\n\n1. **Preflight runtime** — run `task-board q 'project_config()'` before spawning so you know whether this cwd is using a local board or a remote profile and whether `spawn.enabled` permits launches. If it is `false`, do not attempt a spawn or a flag-based workaround; the project policy must be changed first.\n2. **Resolve the agent, then select deliberately for quality per token** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` and the active `spawn.schedule` window. Honor an explicit human/task-context request with `--agent` only when it belongs to the effective set; otherwise omit it so `exclusive` pins its provider or `mixed` retains an in-set runtime affinity. Legacy `preferred_agent` remains a best-effort compatibility path. For the selected agent, assess scope, complexity, risk, required autonomy, validation burden, and expected context and token cost; inspect `task-board q --format compact 'models(agent=<selected-agent>)'`; then choose the strongest suitable explicit model and compatible reasoning effort for Codex or Qwen. Task-board supplies no model or reasoning defaults. A configured `model_criterion: equal` resolves every explicit request to that selected agent's configured model without relying on model ranks.\n3. **Prepare task** — ensure element has clear title, description, scope, AC\n4. **Add task-specific DoD on the board** — checklist items on the element are the task-specific DoD that spawn validates before launch\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Handle edge case X\")'\n   task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260218-abc, text=\"Preserve existing API response shape\")'\n   ```\n5. **Link instruction** (optional) — add precondition resource:\n   ```bash\n   # Inline text instruction\n   task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260218-abc, name=TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md, content=\"...\", type=precondition, description=\"Implementation guide\")'\n\n   # File or URL attachment\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc ./TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md --type precondition\n\n   # Chat attachment only (no local path yet) — requires relux-agents-infra:\n   # https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra\n   agents-attachments materialize\n   task-board resource add TASK-260218-abc \"$(agents-attachments path attachment-001)\" --name TASK-260218-abc_attachment-001.bin --type precondition\n   ```\n6. **Spawn with the effective provider system, affinity, or an in-set explicit override** — after reading `project_config()`, omit `--agent` when `exclusive` should pin its provider or `mixed` contains the current affinity. Pass `--agent` for a human/task-context provider only when the effective set allows it. With no canonical system or legacy preference, `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max` follows runtime affinity and auto-composes the prompt from role + README + checklist DoD + precondition resources\n   - From a Claude-affine orchestrator with no preference, the equivalent shape is `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - With instruction validation: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max --instruction TASK-260218-abc_impl-guide.md`\n   - Explicit override from either runtime: `task-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role reviewer --background --agent claude --model claude-opus-4-8`\n   - Before the first producer launch or delivery status mutation for an approved pool, start its tracked owner: `task-board spawn STORY-260218-auth --role orchestrator --goal-scope TASK-260218-a,TASK-260218-b --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`\n   - A goal-bound Codex owner runs in a dedicated app-server thread created by its tracked runner, regardless of whether the primary session came from `agents-infra codex` or `task-board codex`. The goal is bound there before the first turn. If no task-board caller endpoint is declared, preflight persists `caller_goal_plane=absent` and performs zero caller RPCs; a complete reachable declaration uses the exact snapshot/restore transaction. Partial or unreachable declarations fail closed before spawn side effects. Nested producer/reviewer launches therefore cannot replace the parent delivery condition. Do not replace this with an ordinary prompt-only `codex exec` launch.\n   - A goal-bound child must remain inside the latest active scope. On typed `goal_scope_mismatch`, run the exact revision-bound `task-board spawn goal upsert \"$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID\" --if-revision ... --add-scope ... --reason ...` remediation and retry; never silently absorb work.\n   - A declared parent run context never downgrades silently. While Claude `successor_required` is pending, nested spawn fails side-effect-free with `goal_provider_binding_pending`; if the declared run cannot be loaded, it fails as `goal_parent_context_unavailable`. Retry only from the active rebound successor context.\n7. **Capture the run id** — tracked background spawn returns `RUN-...`; use it as the coordinator handle for that child execution\n   - CLI output now prints non-blocking monitor commands with a seeded cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and preserves the active `task-board` executable / board context\n   - JSON output exposes the same self-contained contract under `observation` (`cursor`, `eventsCommand`, `watchCommand`, `waitCommand`, `runtime`, `delivery`, `inSessionDelivery`)\n8. **Monitor** — `task-board spawn status RUN-...`, `task-board spawn events RUN-... --cursor ...`, `task-board spawn watch RUN-...`, `task-board spawn wait RUN-...`, and `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n   - Directive plane: `task-board spawn directives RUN-...` to inspect durable operator directives and `task-board spawn directive RUN-... <kind>` to request `cancel`, `pause_after_checkpoint`, `restart`, `reroute_after_checkpoint`, `request_progress`, or `nudge`\n   - Goal plane: `task-board spawn goal RUN-...` before nested spawn, at directive checkpoints, and before success claims. It returns the current objective, resolved leaf scope, and optimistic revision.\n   - Owner-agent upsert exposes no goal kind or predicate flags. Creation uses `--scope`; updates require `--if-revision` and use only `--add-scope` and/or `--objective-intent`, with a reason for every non-idempotent revision. The mutation response includes `previous_revision`: `0` for creation, the replaced active revision for a change, and the current revision for an idempotent replay.\n   - If Claude reports `successor_required` for a goal created by an already-running unbound owner, stop normally at the next safe checkpoint. The tracked runner creates and starts the rebound native-`/goal` successor automatically; the predecessor is superseded, never accepted delivery, and cannot launch nested work while rebinding is pending.\n   - Restart and same-semantics reroute are owner-agent recovery actions that retain the active immutable revision. Operator-only cancellation requires a non-empty reason. Structural reroute uses `reroute_after_checkpoint --goal-scope ... --goal-kind ... --goal-success-predicate ... --goal-objective-intent ... --payload ...`; role compatibility is validated before directive persistence.\n9. **Review via child run** — when a producer sets `to-review`, immediately spawn and monitor a reviewer with explicit model selection; never hand routine review to the human\n10. **Own rework through acceptance** — on changes requested, preserve the verdict as rework context, spawn the appropriate producer, wait for a new `to-review`, and spawn another reviewer. Repeat until accepted `done`\n11. **Recover early exits and parked states** — inspect run events and outcome evidence, then retry or reroute through a focused child; mark `blocked` only when the evidence proves a genuine stop-the-line boundary\n12. **Escalate only a real boundary** — ask the human only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision; first record the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
