{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '994,1168p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\nsed -n '1680,1810p' .task-board/.resources/TASK-260720-1nvomm/TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture-v6.md\"","aggregated_output":"#### 6.6.2 Manager parsing and graph proof\n\nFor both network and local sources, manager code—not Git pretty-printers—uses\none byte-level semantic parser after object-ID recomputation. Let `H` be 40\nlowercase hexadecimal bytes for SHA-1 and 64 for SHA-256. The parser applies\nthe same limits and result codes regardless of acquisition path.\n\nThe common **header-record grammar** is:\n\n1. An object is bounded before parsing and contains no NUL byte anywhere.\n2. Its header block uses LF only. CR is forbidden before the header/body\n   separator. The separator is the first exact `LF LF` and is required even\n   when the message is empty. A lone final header LF is not enough.\n3. A primary header line is\n   `<key> SP <value> LF`. `key` is one or more ASCII letters, digits, or `-`,\n   begins with an ASCII letter, and has no normalization. `value` is a bounded\n   byte string with no LF, CR, or NUL and may be empty only for an\n   implementation-ignored extra header.\n4. Zero or more continuation lines may follow an extra header only. Each is\n   `SP <continuation-bytes> LF`; the continuation bytes may be empty and\n   contain no LF, CR, or NUL. A continuation before the first header or after a\n   required structural header is invalid. This admits ordinary `gpgsig`,\n   `gpgsig-sha256`, and `mergetag` records without interpreting or verifying\n   their signatures.\n5. Bytes after the separator are a bounded opaque message. They may contain\n   LF, CR, non-UTF-8 bytes, and signature armor, but not NUL. Message bytes\n   never select a ref, tree, target, cache key field, credential, or process.\n\nA selected **commit** MUST match this ordered grammar:\n\n1. The first record is exactly `tree <H-hex>` with no continuation.\n2. Zero or more `parent <H-hex>` records follow contiguously, each without a\n   continuation. Their byte order is retained as object semantics; the source\n   snapshot does not traverse parent history. Repeated parent IDs are allowed\n   because they do not select compiler input.\n3. Exactly one non-empty `author <opaque-ident>` record and then exactly one\n   non-empty `committer <opaque-ident>` record follow, both without\n   continuation. Their bounded values are metadata only; the manager does not\n   reinterpret names, email, time, timezone, or signature trust.\n4. Zero or more extra records may follow the committer and may use the common\n   continuation grammar. An extra record MUST NOT use any reserved key\n   `tree`, `parent`, `author`, or `committer`; this makes a duplicate or\n   misplaced structural header invalid rather than implementation-selected.\n   Non-reserved extra keys may repeat and remain ordered opaque metadata.\n5. The exact separator follows the last record. The one tree ID is the only\n   commit field used to select snapshot content.\n\nA selected **annotated tag** MUST match this ordered grammar:\n\n1. The first record is exactly `object <H-hex>` and the second is exactly\n   `type <commit-or-tag>`, neither with a continuation.\n2. The third is exactly `tag <safe-tag-name>` with no continuation. It uses\n   section 3.2's byte grammar; the outermost tag record selected through an\n   exact tag ref MUST name that requested tag. Nested tag names are validated\n   but need not equal the outer ref.\n3. At most one non-empty `tagger <opaque-ident>` record may follow, without a\n   continuation. Early tag objects may omit it.\n4. Zero or more non-reserved extra records may then use the common\n   continuation grammar. `object`, `type`, `tag`, and `tagger` are reserved\n   and cannot reappear or occur out of order. Signed tag armor belongs to the\n   opaque message after the separator and is accepted without verification.\n5. After requesting and independently recomputing `object`, the actual\n   `cat-file` type MUST byte-equal the tag's declared `type`. `tag` continues\n   peeling; `commit` terminates it. A `tree` or `blob` target is invalid for\n   this driver. The chain contains at most 16 annotated tag objects, and no\n   full object ID may repeat. Lightweight refs point directly to a commit and\n   consume zero tag-depth slots.\n\nAny malformed commit/tag header, wrong width/case, duplicate or misplaced\nreserved header, bad/missing separator, unexpected/declared type mismatch,\ntag cycle, or tag-depth overflow fails the single stable pre-audit error:\n\n```text\nbuild_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\n```\n\nThe manager maps the common semantic parser to this result on both network and\nlocal-store paths. A network fetch may reject corrupt bytes earlier through\nthe already required transfer fsck, but it MUST NOT turn a known object\nsemantic rejection into availability, cache-hit, or audit success.\n\nAfter a terminal commit passes:\n\n1. the manager requests its one tree ID, recomputes it, and requires actual\n   type `tree`;\n2. it parses each tree as the Git binary sequence\n   `<octal-mode> SP <name-bytes> NUL <H-byte-object-id>`, consuming the entire\n   object with no empty/duplicate name, slash, NUL, `.`/`..`, invalid UTF-8,\n   nonportable component, or platform collision;\n3. it accepts only tree mode `40000` and blob modes `100644`/`100755`; symlink\n   `120000`, gitlink `160000`, unknown modes, and mode/type mismatch fail;\n4. it reads and recomputes every reachable tree and blob, proving every\n   required object locally available without replace, graft, alternate,\n   promisor, lazy-fetch, checkout, attribute, filter, textconv, mailmap, or\n   archive transformation;\n5. it runs section 6.6.3's all-blob detector before materialization or audit;\n6. it materializes exact verified blob bytes into a frozen regular-file\n   snapshot with manager filesystem code, preserving only the executable bit\n   distinction from `100755`/`100644`.\n\nGitlinks fail before reading `.gitmodules`. Parent history, human identity,\ncommit/tag messages, signatures, and extra headers remain untrusted bounded\nmetadata and are never used as provenance.\n\nRequired cross-language semantic fixtures use UTF-8 only for compact display;\nthe parser itself is byte-oriented:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"valid-commit-with-signed-and-extra-headers\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"tree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\nparent bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\nauthor A U Thor <a@example.test> 1 +0000\\ncommitter C O Mitter <c@example.test> 2 +0000\\ngpgsig -----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\\n payload\\n -----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\\nx-curator-fixture opaque\\n\\nmessage\\r\\nbody\\n\",\n    \"expected\": \"valid\",\n    \"tree\": \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"reject-duplicate-tree-header\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"tree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\ntree bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\nauthor A <a@b> 1 +0000\\ncommitter C <c@d> 2 +0000\\n\\nmessage\\n\",\n    \"expected_error\": \"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"reject-misordered-tree-after-parent\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"parent bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\ntree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\nauthor A <a@b> 1 +0000\\ncommitter C <c@d> 2 +0000\\n\\nmessage\\n\",\n    \"expected_error\": \"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"reject-missing-header-message-separator\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"tree aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\nauthor A <a@b> 1 +0000\\ncommitter C <c@d> 2 +0000\\nmessage\\n\",\n    \"expected_error\": \"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"valid-signed-annotated-tag\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"tag\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"object aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\ntype commit\\ntag v1.4.0\\ntagger T Agger <t@example.test> 3 +0000\\n\\nrelease\\n-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\\nopaque\\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\\n\",\n    \"resolved_target_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"expected\": \"valid\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"reject-duplicate-object-and-type-headers\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"tag\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"object aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\nobject bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb\\ntype commit\\ntype tag\\ntag v1.4.0\\n\\nmessage\\n\",\n    \"resolved_target_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"expected_error\": \"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"reject-tag-declared-target-type-mismatch\",\n    \"source_paths\": [\"network-private-store\", \"local-inert-copy\"],\n    \"object_type\": \"tag\",\n    \"object_format\": \"sha1\",\n    \"content_utf8\": \"object aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa\\ntype tag\\ntag v1.4.0\\n\\nmessage\\n\",\n    \"resolved_target_type\": \"commit\",\n    \"expected_error\": \"build_repository_git_object_semantics_invalid\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\n#### 6.6.3 Exact all-blob Git LFS rejection\n\nThe manager scans every reachable blob exactly once, regardless of path,\n- Kotlin/JVM: Maven/Gradle, annotation processors, and compiler plugins are rejected; a direct driver owns class/module path, packaging, and JDK/Kotlin identity.\n- C/C++: Make/CMake/Meson/Ninja are rejected; a direct driver owns sources, preprocessing, headers/sysroot, response files, compiler/linker plugins, native libraries, and output.\n- .NET: MSBuild, analyzers, and source generators are rejected; a direct driver owns references, runtime packaging, native host, and signing.\n\nAdding an external repository does not make an unsafe build frontend safe.\n\n## 13. Threat model\n\n### 13.1 Adversary-controlled\n\n- consuming skill and manifest;\n- declared Git URL, refs/tags served by the remote, object bytes, paths/modes, descriptor, source/vendor/embed inputs, sizes, diagnostics, and compiled artifact;\n- source repository configuration and object-database indirection until rejected;\n- candidate snapshot/receipt/marker/artifact cache bytes until protected admission;\n- network failure, replay, moved tag, malicious remote, and compiler resource-exhaustion input.\n\n### 13.2 Trusted computing base\n\n- manager/protocol implementation;\n- operator-selected Git distribution, HTTPS/SSH transport, credential and host-verification broker;\n- operating system, filesystem containment, locks, protected caches, and transaction journal;\n- operator-trusted Go toolchain and fingerprint;\n- canonical JSON and hash implementations;\n- any future separately standardized signer and opaque signing identity.\n\n### 13.3 Required controls\n\n| Threat | Control |\n|---|---|\n| Mutable/symbolic revision | Full object-format commit lock; optional safe tag must peel exactly |\n| Declared/effective substitution confusion | Both states and substitution type in receipt-v2 input/cache key |\n| Replace refs or grafts | Reject refs/grafts and use `--no-replace-objects` |\n| Partial clone or lazy fetch | Reject promisor state and use `--no-lazy-fetch`; prove all tree/blob objects local |\n| Alternates/object-store escape | Reject alternate files/env and manager-copy into private object DB |\n| Fetch default or tag fallback widens state/process graph | Exact fresh-repo init/fetch argv; no-tag declarations use only the full lock, tagged declarations use only the exact tag ref; fixed destination; no auto-tags, branches, submodules, `FETCH_HEAD`, maintenance, commit graph, refmap, filter, or server option |\n| Ambient config/URL rewrite/proxy/helper | Empty global/system config, manager-owned repo, fixed config/env/process graph |\n| SSH MITM, system config, or variant argv | `GIT_SSH_VARIANT=ssh`; exact wrapper shape; `-F` empty config; operator-known hosts; fixed OpenSSH argv |\n| Local repository extension/ref ambiguity | Admit only ordinary files-ref layout; byte-level config/ref parser; reject gitfile, commondir, bare, reftable, or unknown extensions |\n| Malicious/incomplete pack metadata | Pack v2/v3 plus index-v2 admission, hash-family filenames/trailers, inert private copying, fixed trusted `cat-file --batch`, and manager object-ID recomputation |\n| Object-reader transformation or process escape | One full-OID-only batch grammar; no `ls-tree`, revision/path input, filter, textconv, mailmap, alternate, lazy fetch, or object-reader child |\n| Commit/tag parser disagreement | One ordered manager byte grammar, reserved-header uniqueness, exact separator, actual target-type checks, and one stable semantic error on both source paths |\n| Hook/filter/LFS/submodule execution | Raw object extraction; no checkout/archive transforms; pinned Git LFS 3.7.1-family conservative all-blob detector; reject gitlinks |\n| Package output/argv/signing selection | Strict descriptor; command-key output; closed driver; no signing fields |\n| Audit bypass on cache hit | Exact snapshot validation/hash/audit before artifact-cache lookup |\n| Forged cache | Protected boundary plus exact digests; rebuild rather than adopt |\n| Shim/PATH hijack | Structural no-follow/hash validation; manager-generated shims; never execute output |\n| Partial/cross-project install | One manager-home journaled transaction; consumer last; reverse rollback |\n\nSame-principal/admin compromise remains outside the local protected-cache invariant. Receipt and marker hashes are consistency/currentness identifiers, not signatures.\n\n## 14. Exact impact on existing board work\n\nThis scope is a new schema-7/rc.5 delivery subtree. It MUST NOT reopen, relabel, or silently expand rc.4 work.\n\n### 14.1 Existing curator-spec schema-6 story\n\n`STORY-260720-35dck7` (“Protocol schema v6”) and all 12 child tasks remain rc.4 evidence. In particular:\n\n- `TASK-260720-1nvomm`, `TASK-260720-17llva`, and `TASK-260720-wajgn8` remain the schema-6 core/profile/manifest contract;\n- `TASK-260720-12iigs`, `TASK-260720-2zc6k1`, and `TASK-260720-37ei85` remain receipt-v1/marker-v2, claim-v2, and legacy guards;\n- `TASK-260720-1s1vr6`, `TASK-260720-cw39jh`, `TASK-260720-1u7hes`, `TASK-260720-3lo9jc`, `TASK-260720-q5oy3o`, and `TASK-260720-3ag6pi` remain rc.4 vectors, gates, docs, release metadata, and integrated verification.\n\nNo existing schema-6 task should absorb schema 7. After architecture acceptance, the smallest new curator-spec set is:\n\n1. core/security/decision contract;\n2. wire schemas and generated schema cases;\n3. manager profile/CLI including Git, substitutions, status/repair/GC;\n4. conformance/release gates including raw-object, mixed-build, and claim-v3 vectors.\n\nTask-local tests/docs stay with those deliverables; no ceremonial extra task is justified.\n\n### 14.2 Existing Curator implementation story\n\n`STORY-260720-3plyvy` (“Curator Go build driver”) remains the local schema-6 `go-v1` implementation. Its manifest, source identity, toolchain, receipt/cache, marker, shim, transaction, lifecycle, docs, and rc.4 verification tasks are prerequisites/reusable foundations, not owners of external Git semantics.\n\nA later external-repository Curator story consumes those foundations and adds:\n\n- schema-7/repository/descriptor/substitution models;\n- clean Git acquisition and raw-object snapshot admission;\n- independent external audit subjects;\n- receipt v2, marker v3, mixed-command planning, snapshot-cache currentness/GC;\n- end-to-end rc.5 vectors.\n\nDo not change `TASK-260720-1zntv0` or other `go-v1` tasks to accept Git fields or arbitrary build behavior.\n\n### 14.3 Existing cocoaskills implementation story\n\n`STORY-260720-1uv5gi` (“csk Go build driver”) likewise remains the independent local schema-6 implementation. A new external-repository story should build on its schema model, transaction engine, source/toolchain identities, protected caches, planner, currentness/repair/GC, docs, and E2E gates.\n\nThe new work owns Python-specific clean Git/raw-object handling, external audit subjects, receipt-v2/marker-v3 mixed builds, substitutions, and rc.5 vectors. Existing `git archive` behavior is not sufficient unless independently proven byte-identical and free of attributes/filters; the recommended implementation is raw object extraction.\n\n### 14.4 Existing interoperability story\n\n`STORY-260720-21bsr2` (“Compiled-build interoperability”) remains rc.4/schema-6 evidence. Its 12 tasks, including shared compiled cases, native consumers, black-box runner, authoring guide, language-driver gate, release qualification, and pins, MUST NOT claim schema 7.\n\nAfter both managers implement schema 7, a downstream rc.5 interoperability extension adds exact cross-language comparisons for:\n\n- canonical URL/ref parsing and SHA-1/SHA-256 locks;\n- raw snapshot bytes/digest and declared/effective identities;\n- unsubstituted/substituted/mixed receipt and marker bytes;\n- unavailable, moved, alternate/replace/graft/promisor, helper/filter/LFS/submodule negatives;\n- cache hit/miss/corruption, status/repair/GC, shim/PATH, rollback, and release evidence.\n\nLinux remains a separate later native-validation item and cannot be listed in claim v3 until it passes.\n\n## 15. Rejected alternatives\n\n- Add Git/ref/output fields to a build command: duplicates source declarations and reopens output control.\n- Let the descriptor name a binary/output: violates manager-derived output and collision/path safety.\n- Reuse `go-v1`: changes source acquisition, identity, receipt, and marker meaning under an rc.4 driver.\n- Tag-only, branch, abbreviated ID, or raw revision grammar: mutable or ambiguous compiler input.\n- Trust a source or artifact cache without exact protected admission and audit: bypasses source policy.\n- Permit replacements, alternates, grafts, partial clones, lazy fetch, local upload-pack, submodules, or LFS: adds unbound objects, processes, credentials, or network.\n- Use `git clone`, an implicit/default fetch, or a named remote: admits template/config/refspec/tag/submodule/maintenance behavior not selected by the manager.\n- Fetch the full locked object ID before verifying a declared tag, or use either\n  form as fallback for the other: permits a successful operation and receipt\n  without proving the separately promised tag assertion. Tagged declarations\n  use only the exact tag ref; untagged declarations use only the full lock.\n- Inherit Git/SSH/proxy/helper/filter configuration: transfers process/network control outside the closed manager policy.\n- Auto-detect the SSH variant or pass the source SSH configuration: permits variant-dependent argv and system/user proxy, command, identity, forwarding, or control behavior.\n- Admit gitfiles, linked worktrees, bare repositories, reftable, unknown repository extensions, optional pack sidecars, pack versions outside 2/3, or pack indexes outside v2 in local-substitution v1: requires additional containment/parser semantics and vectors; reject with stable errors instead.\n- Let each manager choose its own documented pack/index subset: makes the same local substitution non-interoperable; v1 fixes pack 2/3 plus index 2.\n- Use `ls-tree`, `--batch-command`, pretty-printers, revision/path expressions, checkout, archive, filters, textconv, or mailmap for extraction: adds redundant grammars or repository-selected transformations; one full-OID `cat-file --batch` stream is sufficient.\n- Let `cat-file` type labels choose among malformed commit/tag headers: local raw objects can contain duplicate or misordered structural headers; the manager applies one exact semantic grammar after recomputing IDs.\n- Treat recognized Git LFS pointer text as compiler source: a package can make a pointer visible through `go:embed` while the actual bytes remain outside the locked Git graph; v1 rejects every form accepted by the pinned Git LFS 3.7.1 parser family before audit.\n- Store only effective substitution state: loses declared provenance; store only declared state: misdescribes compiled bytes.\n- Upgrade every schema-7 command to receipt v2: unnecessarily changes local `go-v1`; mixed explicit versions preserve rc.4 semantics.\n- Install-time signing/notarization: introduces secrets, post-processing children, mutable services, and new identity not covered by the first driver.\n- Standardize physical cache/checkouts: machine-local layout remains implementation-specific.\n\n## 16. Primary-source register\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"agent_message","text":"The independent smoke now matches all six required outcomes on Git 2.50.1: matching annotated tags resolve under both direct-OID policies; moved tags resolve to a different terminal commit and are classified as `build_repository_ref_moved`; missing tags fail fetch and map to `build_repository_source_unavailable`; every fresh repository contains only `refs/curator/tag`, and none writes `FETCH_HEAD`. All 13 JSON examples parse, and the pinned validation environment passes 30 schemas, 93 vectors, 8 Python tests, and the Go tool tests."}}
